dunit‚dmarkf200000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;uVFz-4oT8kOSRSLUjejA0qqt9RWJGDzGtaPz-Z83IcAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarks0.22254060000000003ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QpcYHZFneg9ErXYTfvSayiiRbGlGCAbaR6OhN--J2Agƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WzH6oycmhP2CbgNgYhpMz4E4OW2zF07RBndiwVdzEVUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hB_HBkM2dsga5d04G-DC3jCJ-jIawak6mrKCMJ5AlUoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd7342ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ng_OtgG5ZNbEiUlftqahXjIoA9liRpO2gmVNirCIpscƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;RoenQu7aocA-OXsfpryz2y5Xx6blhf0yz1stIsyHbmgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AVytup6XsFqUrgkAApeAekEijtmos48Xw9N0ehBCmugƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Wm966L_9bjO7TuR8ZmFfV4oMGD5QrxAZO_mRpFD5ac4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky‘Mozilla plans to cut its Firefox release interval in half, moving from a four-week cycle to one release every two weeks beginning in September 2026. Sylvestre Ledru, Mozilla's director of engineering, announced the change on the dev-platform@mozilla.org mailing list, as [reported by The Register on July 17, 2026](https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423).

Ledru's announcement framed the change as a trial rather than a permanent commitment. "We are planning to move Firefox Desktop and Android from a 4-week release cadence to a 2-week release cadence starting in September 2026," he wrote, according to The Register. "This will be an experiment." He also specified that the shorter gap between releases does not obligate engineering teams to ship twice as much work in the same time. "This does not mean that all work needs to ship twice as fast. Work that is not ready should not be rushed, and features can still take the time they need to bake," Ledru wrote, as The Register quoted him. He stated that Mozilla would monitor the change closely and would adjust if the experiment revealed problems.

The shift is already reflected in the Firefox Release Calendar. At the time of The Register's reporting, the calendar still showed the existing four-week cadence for Firefox 153 and 154, but placed Firefox 155 on September 1, 2026 â€” two weeks earlier than its previously scheduled September 15 date. From Firefox 155 forward, the calendar lists releases at intervals of approximately two weeks.

## A Cadence Mozilla Has Compressed Before

Mozilla is not attempting this kind of acceleration for the first time. The Register notes that a comparable schedule change occurred more than a decade ago. The browser market has seen similar moves elsewhere: The Register reported Google's transition to six-weekly Chrome releases in 2010 and to four-weekly releases in 2021, and Google announced a new cadence change for Chrome earlier this year as well.

## Firefox 153 Arrives First, With ESR Designation

The cadence change does not affect Extended Support Release versions, which remain on an annual cycle. The next ESR release happens to be close at hand: Firefox 153 is scheduled for Tuesday, July 21, 2026, according to The Register. Because Firefox 153 carries the next ESR designation, The Register reports it will receive security patches for a minimum of fifteen months, with that support window running at least through late 2027. For comparison, the source notes that Firefox 115, an earlier ESR, is still receiving updates and will continue to do so until March 2027.

The ESR designation also carries a downstream consequence: Firefox 153 forms the codebase basis for the current beta of Waterfox, an AI-free fork, which has recently incorporated built-in ad blocking, according to The Register.

## What Firefox 153 Adds

The Register's review of the Firefox 153 beta release notes describes an extensive feature list. PDF handling sees the most substantial changes: version 153 will allow users to combine multiple PDF documents into one by dragging them into the PDF sidebar, and to insert images as standalone new pages within an existing PDF. Text highlighting within PDF documents also improves.

A new QR code generator lets users produce a scannable code for any web page, a capability The Register suggests is particularly useful for sharing links in printed documents. The release also adds support for the EU's Qualified Website Authentication Certificates â€” QWACs, defined under the eIDAS framework, whose full title is "electronic IDentification, Authentication and trust Services" â€” allowing Firefox 153 to verify and display them.

A security change alters the default behavior of extensions: in Firefox 153, extensions will no longer have access to local files unless a user explicitly grants that permission. The Register notes that tools dependent on this capability â€” its coverage cites the Multithreaded Download Manager as a specific example â€” will require users to reinstate the access on a per-extension basis.

Interface additions include a map-pin icon in the address bar when a tab is actively drawing on device location data. Typed commands in the address bar expand on audio-muting controls introduced in the previous release, gaining functions for color selection from pages and access to experimental "Labs" features. Experimental support for the JPEG XL image format is also included.

Platform-specific improvements round out the release. Windows users with a compatible GPU and the appropriate drivers can expect HDR video playback in version 153. On macOS, Fn+F now enters full-screen mode. Picture-in-picture video controls have been refined across platforms.

## Whether the Experiment Holds

With Firefox 155 moved to September 1 in the release calendar and subsequent versions listed at two-week intervals, the mechanics of the shift are already set. The remaining uncertainty, as The Register's coverage makes clear, is whether Mozilla's monitoring confirms that biweekly delivery is sustainable or prompts the adjustment Ledru's announcement explicitly left open. That answer will not arrive until the accelerated schedule is running.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ciU3QbV55tBPq7GbfLkzOkS3NJPf8spwUx8KF2LKDq4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$5bc509d9-b8e7-49f4-9bdd-cff972223b72ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fMl6LW6BpDezh7SG1gAjFm3g0z6m99kXUqFloLE24Zwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhend_turnƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_P-JHLqYasxk2uMkz3m-kZAtw6JN-DeGqXPZhqan438ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgsuccessƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fraOtGJJRdcqEqdZD_oNLAQi0Ek1YBYZdZV33JZ6dlcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkicompletedƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_mR5W58xeRJdsFWWIrtuf9soNAakD-mBmPcFSusxq9sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkc284ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1c1L-1cUhYyPRkiTFvVKE_Fz-ZnCmnRmZJ6miCINKh0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarks0.24146860000000003ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ulemfas15l8blkZD-kV22p9PWkB4xlcWOAwQGQ65DdYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf116050ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;oFI_3H7CcDcebUqZfr97J5bQNk-kGMDNHR6CBF4ToQMƒdpairƒer