Do not invent connective tissue, causal claims, quotations, identities, numbers, temporal order, policy contents, or geography. If the packet cannot support a requested expansion, narrow or remove the claim.

Every factual paragraph must carry a normal reader-facing source link or an immediately continuing attribution. Never reproduce internal claim IDs or claim tokens.

lib/management/offices/reporter.json:
{"kind":"koios.office","id":"reporter","incumbent":"Koios reporting mechanism with contracted prose inference","authority":["accept an assignment and bounded research packet","procure prose inference from the cheapest faithful admitted offer","form a manuscript under the selected genre contract","refuse when the reporting packet cannot sustain the assignment or genre"],"duties":["forming-a-reported-manuscript"],"separation":"May draft but cannot admit its own sources, accept its own work, publish, correct an already published article, or grant provider authority."}

lib/management/offices/editor-in-chief.json:
{"kind":"koios.office","id":"editor-in-chief","incumbent":"Nik Koios","authority":["adopt and amend newsroom standards subject to the Leader's mandate","turn audience or public-interest demand into assignments","accept or refuse provider offers for research, reporting, writing, and independent editorial judgment","authorize publication only after exact-manuscript acceptance"],"duties":["judging-accountable-publication","learning-from-newsroom-outcomes"],"separation":"May authorize editorial publication but may not silently grant delivery infrastructure authority or mutate a provider's territory."}

lib/management/duties/forming-a-reported-manuscript.json:
{"kind":"koios.duty","id":"forming-a-reported-manuscript","office":"reporter","precondition":"A durable assignment names one supported demand lens, one event spine, a genre contract, and a bounded packet of admitted source evidence.","move":"Route prose work to the cheapest faithful provider, form one event-centered manuscript, and preserve exact source links, attribution, modality, scope, production provenance, and refusal evidence.","postcondition":"A draft linked to the assignment exists for independent editorial judgment, or a typed reporting/source/provider obstruction is recorded.","refusals":["unsupported assignment lens","no event spine","insufficient distinct reported detail","provider offer absent","provider output malformed","citation custody lost"]}

lib/management/duties/judging-accountable-publication.json:
{"kind":"koios.duty","id":"judging-accountable-publication","office":"editor-in-chief","precondition":"One exact manuscript, its deterministic standards reading, source and claim records, production provenance, and any prior failure evidence are available.","move":"Procure independent judgment from an accepted provider under a provider-validated bounded tool schema, preserve its prose report, and let deterministic Koios code invoke exactly one selected editorial tool. Free-form prose labels never create publication authority.","postcondition":"The exact manuscript has an append-only accepted, revise, or refuse judgment carrying the validated tool-selection digest; publication is possible only when submit_editorial_acceptance was selected for deterministically clear bytes.","refusals":["missing source custody","missing provider offer","provider result not accepted","manuscript digest changed","standards gate failed"]}

lib/management/contracts/liss-routed-writer-inference.json:
{"kind":"koios.bilateral-contract-interface","id":"koios-hires-liss-for-routed-writer-inference","customer":"urn:ame:koios-news","provider":"urn:ame:logical-inference-service-section","providerContract":"liss-inference-service","service":"cheapest-sufficient-reported-prose","admission":"Koios solicits the strict forward subset of free-groq, free-nim, and leader-claude for which an invocation-scoped account grant is actually injected; LISS authors the exact effects and route offer; Koios accepts only that offer with an attenuated capability.","packet":["assignment","reported-analysis genre law","bounded admitted research packet","prose output shape","replayable minimum sufficiency predicates"],"result":"Provider-authored routed completion or typed refusal, with selected tier and each provider attempt retained in provider and Koios ledgers.","authority":"Provider credentials are not Koios property. The Leader Movement Team must expressly seat account custody and grant one invocation; environment presence alone is neither custody nor authorization.","editorialSeparation":"A writer-tier completion creates only a draft. Exact-manuscript publication still requires the separately contracted leader-Claude editorial judgment.","status":"leader-claude-executing; free-groq-interface-ready-awaiting-leader-custody-and-grant; free-nim-interface-ready-awaiting-invocation-grant"}

lib/law/accountable-attention-and-publication.json:
{"kind":"koios.publication-law","id":"accountable-attention-and-publication","purpose":"Compete for useful attention without allowing demand, compression, generation, advertising, or delivery to manufacture warrant.","invariants":["Demand may create an assignment but never evidence.","A source statement remains attributable to its source until an authorized editorial act adopts a narrower newsroom claim.","No public surface may create a factual commitment not licensed by the article, its cited evidence, its uncertainty, or a declared editorial stance.","A manuscript is not publishable unless deterministic gates clear and the latest exact-manuscript independent editorial judgment accepts it.","Corrections, clarifications, updates, and retractions must propagate to every dependent headline, article, feed, card, sitemap, metadata record, and syndication surface that Koios controls.","Audience demand, attention, advertising, revenue, provider cost, and editorial warrant are recorded as distinct facts and never substituted for one another.","Newsroom learning may propose a replay, route review, observation period, or standards review from durable outcomes; it may not itself publish, execute the replay, alter a route, or amend law.","LLMs may produce prose, images, or bounded choices; deterministic code owns serialization, digests, admission, ledgers, and publication transitions.","A provider is selected by the cheapest route that satisfies the declared quality and evidence requirements; zero marginal price never waives those requirements.","No deployment target becomes authoritative without parity evidence, an accepted resource grant, a rollback route, and an explicit cutover act.","A public privacy surface must describe the requests the exact publication artifact actually emits; suspended advertising or attention may not be described as active, and active third-party carriers may not be described as absent.","Advertising units, placements, provider requests, seller declarations, performance receipts, and revenue enter Koios only under provider-authored or provider-accepted evidence; Koios never invents ads.txt authority."],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/claims-to-prose.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/news-topology.md"],"enforcedBy":["src/researching/writing/editing/standards.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/liss-editorial.mts","src/management/newsroom-learning.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/publishing/managing/circulating/index.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-shadow.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-profile-parity.mts"]}

lib/law/reported-analysis-genre-contract.json:
{"kind":"koios.genre-contract","id":"reported-analysis/v1","promise":"A timely report organized around one witnessed event, with Koios's systems interpretation visibly subordinate to and licensed by the reporting.","evidenceBudget":{"targetWords":{"minimum":800,"maximum":1100},"minimumDistinctAssertions":15,"minimumPrimaryAssertions":15,"maximumWordsPerAssertion":55,"law":"The requested length is licensed only when the admitted packet carries enough distinct source-bound assertions to sustain it. When the budget does not clear, acquire reporting or commission a narrower genre before writer inference."},"writerPromptLaws":["Write reported analysis, not a thematic essay: the event and the people, institutions, places, dates, quantities, documents, and decisions in the sources remain the article's spine.","Before drafting, silently inventory the packet's supported claims and distinct new details; every paragraph and every section must advance at least one of them rather than restating the frame.","Treat each source as evidence only for its own actors, place, time, scope, attribution, and modality; never use thematic similarity as a bridge between different events.","Koios's systems interpretation may explain a pattern made visible by supported facts, but it may not supply an unreported causal mechanism, motive, consequence, category, or analogy.","Use straight attribution when the source is an allegation, estimate, forecast, interpretation, institutional statement, or contested claim; do not launder it into Koios's voice.","Model output is never evidence. Prefer original and named sources, preserve source motives and uncertainty, and treat consequential claims as publishable only to the extent they are corroborated by the admitted packet.","When the story criticizes a person or institution, alleges wrongdoing, or reports a dispute, represent the subject's response or state only what the admitted reporting establishes about a documented opportunity to respond; otherwise return REPORTING_NEEDED.","Avoid needless harm, sensationalism, stereotypes, unattributed opinion, and commercial influence; keep reported analysis visibly distinct from advocacy and advertising.","A headline may intensify relevance but not certainty; it names the subject before withholding an answer and preserves material attribution, uncertainty, scope, and correction state.","The lede tells the reader what happened, where, to whom, and why it matters now; do not open with a universal thesis, scene-setting abstraction, or generic collapse language.","Do not create a section unless the packet supplies a distinct factual development for it; if fewer than three sections are supportable, refuse with REPORTING_NEEDED instead of padding.","The ending must close on the most consequential supported unresolved fact, decision, deadline, conflict, or question facing the named actors; never substitute an abstract meditation for reporting.","Give readers enough factual payoff in the headline, description, summary, and lede to make an informed click or no-click decision; withhold an answer when useful, never the subject."],"gates":["event spine","paragraph-level source warrant","attribution and modality preservation","headline-body congruity","non-repetition","correction readiness","exact-manuscript independent editorial acceptance"],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/journalism.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/circulation-surface.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/clickbait.md"]}

DETERMINISTIC SOURCE-ORIGINALITY GATE REPORT:
The deterministic source-originality gate found 1 source-shaped sentence(s).
Replay the writer against the same witnessed source packet. Use materially independent prose or an explicit, properly attributed quotation. Preserve all other warranted claims and citations, and do not refresh or replace the sources.
1. Do not reuse this draft sentence: This version will serve as the next ESR iteration, providing security updates for at least 15 months.
   Reconstruct its supported facts independently from https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423 or use a short explicit quotation.

WITNESSED RESEARCH PACKET:
{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet","generatedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:45.772Z","term":"mozilla speeds firefox release schedule biweekly","beat":"technology","queryTerms":["mozilla","speeds","firefox","release","schedule","biweekly"],"sourceCount":1,"selectedCount":1,"intakeSources":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly","wire":"technology","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:9a83ef275151120c6c63337cde01f5e6e93960de97e35dcff9f9efdac5663198","title":"Mozilla speeds Firefox release schedule to biweekly","url":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","domain":"theregister.com","published":"2026-07-17T17:40:00.000Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:48.560Z","score":110,"matchedTerms":["mozilla","speeds","firefox","release","schedule","biweekly"],"summary":"Firefox releases will soon get even closer together â€“ but not ESR ones, which remain annual, with the next one due out soon. The change was announced last week on the dev-platform@mozilla.org mailing list by Mozillaâ€™s director of engineering Sylvestre Ledru: â€œWe are planning to move Firefox Desktop and Android from a 4-week release cadence to a 2-week release cadence starting in September 2026. â€œThis will be an experimentâ€¦ This does not mean that all work needs to ship twice as fast. Work that is not ready should not be rushed, and features can still take the time they need to bake. â€œThe current target is to release Firefox 155 on September 1, 2026, instead of September 15 â€¦ â€œWe will closely monitor how this change works in practice and adjust if needed.â€ The change is already visible in the upcoming Firefox Release Calendar. At the time of writing, it shows the current four-week cadence for Firefox 153 and 154, with Firefox 155 brought forward to September 1. From then on, it lists releases at roughly two-week intervals. This isnâ€™t unprecedented. The last time Mozilla did it was over a decade ago. Google announced a very similar change for Chrome back in March, and The Register reported on it going to six-weekly releases in 2010 and then four-weekly ones in 2021. Brace for the impact of the next ESR In the meantime, Firefox 153 is nearly here: at the time of writing, itâ€™s scheduled for Tuesday, July 21. Right now, the release notes are blank, but the beta release notes are more forthcoming. You can look forward to further improvements to PDF editing. Version 153 will be able to merge multiple PDF documents into one by a simple drag-and-drop in the PDF sidebar, and insert images into PDFs as new pages. It will also improve highlighting text in PDFs. The new version can generate QR codes for sharing web pages offline, such as in printed documents. It can verify and display the EUâ€™s new Qualified Website Authentication Certificates, or QWACs for short. These are part of the new eIDAS legislation, which is a slightly convoluted acronym for â€œelectronic IDentification, Authentication and trust Servicesâ€. Sounds cumbersome, but if it helps make the Wild West of the Web a bit safer, weâ€™re all for it. Another security tweak is that extensions will lose local-file access by default. As we use one that relies on it - the excellent Multithreaded Download Manager - that's slightly concerning, although users will be able to grant the permission separately, so we'll see. If a tab is using your location, Firefox 153 will highlight this with a map-pin icon in the address bar. The last release could recognize a few keywords in the address bar to mute all sound: this function will get extended, with typed commands for picking colors from pages, and accessing experimental â€œLabsâ€ features. Come back, Ubuntu Unity and your HUD, all is forgiven. Thereâ€™s also experimental JPEG XL support. Pop-up video player controls will work better, and if you use Windows and have a compatible GPU and drivers, 153 will play HDR video. On macOS, Firefox supports the Fn+F keystroke for switching to full-screen mode â€“ which means KeyboardControls.com has to update its description of the function. Firefox 153 will also be a significant release, because it will be the next ESR version: it will get security updates for at least 15 months, meaning until late 2027. (And maybe longer: Firefox 115 is still getting updates, and now will until March 2027.) In turn, that means that Firefox 153 is also the basis of the latest beta of the AI-free Waterfox fork, which recently integrated built-in ad blocking. Â®"}],"claims":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Mozilla speeds Firefox release schedule to biweekly","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:48.560Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:48.560Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Firefox releases will soon get even closer together â€“ but not ESR ones, which remain annual, with the next one due out soon. The change was announced last week on the dev-platform@mozilla.org mailing list by Mozillaâ€™s director of engineering Sylvestre Ledru: â€œWe are planning to move Firefox Desktop and Android from a 4-week release cadence to a 2-week release cadence starting in September 2026. â€œThis will be an experimentâ€¦ This does not mean that all work needs to ship twice as fast. Work that is not ready should not be rushed, and features can still take the time they need to bake. â€œThe current target is to release Firefox 155 on September 1, 2026, instead of September 15 â€¦ â€œWe will closely monitor how this change works in practice and adjust if needed.â€ The change is already visible in the upcoming Firefox Release Calendar. At the time of writing, it shows the current four-week cadence for Firefox 153 and 154, with Firefox 155 brought forward to September 1. From then on, it lists releases at roughly two-week intervals. This isnâ€™t unprecedented. The last time Mozilla did it was over a decade ago. Google announced a very similar change for Chrome back in March, and The Register reported on it going to six-weekly releases in 2010 and then four-weekly ones in 2021. Brace for the impact of the next ESR In the meantime, Firefox 153 is nearly here: at the time of writing, itâ€™s scheduled for Tuesday, July 21. Right now, the release notes are blank, but the beta release notes are more forthcoming. You can look forward to further improvements to PDF editing. Version 153 will be able to merge multiple PDF documents into one by a simple drag-and-drop in the PDF sidebar, and insert images into PDFs as new pages. It will also improve highlighting text in PDFs. The new version can generate QR codes for sharing web pages offline, such as in printed documents. It can verify and display the EUâ€™s new Qualified Website Authentication Certificates, or QWACs for short. These are part of the new eIDAS legislation, which is a slightly convoluted acronym for â€œelectronic IDentification, Authentication and trust Servicesâ€. Sounds cumbersome, but if it helps make the Wild West of the Web a bit safer, weâ€™re all for it. Another security tweak is that extensions will lose local-file access by default. As we use one that relies on it - the excellent Multithreaded Download Manager - that's slightly concerning, although users will be able to grant the permission separately, so we'll see. If a tab is using your location, Firefox 153 will highlight this with a map-pin icon in the address bar. The last release could recognize a few keywords in the address bar to mute all sound: this function will get extended, with typed commands for picking colors from pages, and accessing experimental â€œLabsâ€ features. Come back, Ubuntu Unity and your HUD, all is forgiven. Thereâ€™s also experimental JPEG XL support. Pop-up video player controls will work better, and if you use Windows and have a compatible GPU and drivers, 153 will play HDR video. On macOS, Firefox supports the Fn+F keystroke for switching to full-screen mode â€“ which means KeyboardControls.com has to update its description of the function. Firefox 153 will also be a significant release, because it will be the next ESR version: it will get security updates for at least 15 months, meaning until late 2027. (And maybe longer: Firefox 115 is still getting updates, and now will until March 2027.) In turn, that means that Firefox 153 is also the basis of the latest beta of the AI-free Waterfox fork, which recently integrated built-in ad blocking. Â®","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:48.560Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Fri, 17 Jul 2026 19:40:00 +0200","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T01:13:48.560Z"}],"warrant":{"status":"research-intake","supportClaimCount":4,"refutationClaimCount":0,"uncertainClaimCount":0,"sourceDomainCount":1,"sourceDomains":["theregister.com"],"note":"Current-provider claims are source-bound research intake. Prior-corpus excerpts are context only and license no public factual claim."},"editorialRisks":[{"code":"demand-is-not-evidence","severity":"note","message":"The assignment and search terms select attention but license no factual claim."},{"code":"prior-research-is-context-only","severity":"note","message":"Recovered prior research may guide reporting but is not promoted into source claims by this service."},{"code":"prior-research-gaps","severity":"warning","message":"Corpus Management reported 7 explicit recovery gaps; do not infer that omitted or unmatched material is absent from the wider corpus."}],"sourceUrls":["https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423"],"candidateReferences":[{"id":"source-0d9767d06b78","type":"webpage","title":"Mozilla speeds Firefox release schedule to biweekly","URL":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/17/mozilla-speeds-firefox-release-schedule-to-biweekly/5274423","container-title":"theregister.com"}],"context":["[1] Mozilla speeds Firefox release schedule to biweekly (theregister.com, 2026-07-17T17:40:00.000Z) - Firefox releases will soon get even closer together â€“ but not ESR ones, which remain annual, with the next one due out soon. The change was announced last week on the dev-platform@mozilla.org mailing list by Mozillaâ€™s director of engineering Sylvestre Ledru: â€œWe are planning to move Firefox Desktop and Android from a 4-week release cadence to a 2-week release cadence starting in September 2026. â€œThis will be an experimentâ€¦ This does not mean that all work needs to ship twice as fast. Work that is not ready should not be rushed, and features can still take the time they need to bake. â€œThe current target is to release Firefox 155 on September 1, 2026, instead of September 15 â€¦ â€œWe will closely monitor how this change works in practice and adjust if needed.â€ The change is already visible in the upcoming Firefox Release Calendar. At the time of writing, it shows the current four-week cadence for Firefox 153 and 154, with Firefox 155 brought forward to September 1. From then on, it lists releases at roughly two-week intervals. This isnâ€™t unprecedented. The last time Mozilla did it was over a decade ago. Google announced a very similar change for Chrome back in March, and The Register reported on it going to six-weekly releases in 2010 and then four-weekly ones in 2021. Brace for the impact of the next ESR In the meantime, Firefox 153 is nearly here: at the time of writing, itâ€™s scheduled for Tuesday, July 21. Right now, the release notes are blank, but the beta release notes are more forthcoming. You can look forward to further improvements to PDF editing. Version 153 will be able to merge multiple PDF documents into one by a simple drag-and-drop in the PDF sidebar, and insert images into PDFs as new pages. It will also improve highlighting text in PDFs. The new version can generate QR codes for sharing web pages offline, such as in printed documents. It can verify and display the EUâ€™s new Qualified Website Authentication Certificates, or QWACs for short. These are part of the new eIDAS legislation, which is a slightly convoluted acronym for â€œelectronic IDentification, Authentication and trust Servicesâ€. Sounds cumbersome, but if it helps make the Wild West of the Web a bit safer, weâ€™re all for it. Another security tweak is that extensions will lose local-file access by default. As we use one that relies on it - the excellent Multithreaded Download Manager - that's slightly concerning, although users will be able to grant the permission separately, so we'll see. If a tab is using your location, Firefox 153 will highlight this with a map-pin icon in the address bar. The last release could recognize a few keywords in the address bar to mute all sound: this function will get extended, with typed commands for picking colors from pages, and accessing experimental â€œLabsâ€ features. Come back, Ubuntu Unity and your HUD, all is forgiven. Thereâ€™s also experimental JPEG XL support. Pop-up video player controls will work better, and if you use Windows and have a compatible GPU and drivers, 153 will play HDR video. On macOS, Firefox supports the Fn+F keystroke for switching to full-screen mode â€“ which means KeyboardControls.com has to update its description of the function. Firefox 153 will also be a significant release, because it will be the next ESR version: it will get security updates for at least 15 months, meaning until late 2027. (And maybe longer: Firefox 115 is still getting updates, and now will until March 2027.) In turn, that means that Firefox 153 is also the basis of the latest beta of the AI-free Waterfox fork, which recently integrated built-in ad blocking. Â®","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Internal planning now has `src/administrating/developing`, exposed in `/departments.json`, human board `/administrating/developing`, `/administrating/developing.json`, OpenAPI, and MCP tool `read_developing`. Its `topologyConstruction` section records the current upstream/content-derived plan for nested component topology: source-to-reference fibration, desk-chain nesting, article sheaf/cosheaf, newsroom swarm hands, semantic hypertext packets, and aperiodic dream budgeting. Semantic hypertext packets are now active for source-cycle repair packets and content revision packets through `.packet.html` routes with scriptless `data-e`/`data-a`/`data-v` cells. Aggregate ad-performance managemen...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Draft-time same-site link guidance now sits before publication: `/operators/writing/internal-link-guidance.json`, `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/link-guidance.json`, MCP tool `read_draft_internal_link_guidance`, research-packet HTML, assignment work-order HTML, and assignment draft forms all expose published Koios article targets to consider while writing. This is composition guidance only: it excludes assignment source article slugs, does not create references, and must not be treated as factual support. - **The source cycle is an operator-visible production act.** `src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts` reads `producing-content.config.json`, ingests Koios-owned newscable sou...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): The Koios wrapper snapshots existing manuscripts before the farm writes and restores any existing path collision, reporting those as `candidateWriteCollisions` so autonomous drafts cannot silently unpublish or overwrite shipped articles. Publication remains standards-gated. Failed candidate gates and intake obstructions now fold into read-only source-cycle repair packets at `/operators/source-cycle/repairs`, `/operators/source-cycle/repairs.json`, per-candidate `/operators/source-cycle/repairs/{slug}` and `/operators/source-cycle/repairs/{slug}.json`, operator workbench `sourceCycleRepairs`, and MCP tools `read_source_cycle_repairs`/`read_source_cycle_repair_packet`; packets preserve the...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): `/operators/manuscripts.json` gives the machine manuscript inventory: draft state, public URL, standards URL, publish URL, next work state, reference/word counts, and blocking findings per manuscript. Human operators have a dedicated manuscript queue at `/operators/manuscripts.html`, sorted by next editorial action while preserving `POST /operators/manuscripts` as the JSON draft-creation endpoint. Drafts are also ranked by publication distance at `/operators/publication-candidates` and `/operators/publication-candidates.json`, with workbench field `publicationCandidates` and MCP tool `read_publication_candidates`; this is a projection over standards reviews and source-cycle repair packets...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Human operators have a dedicated manuscript queue at `/operators/manuscripts.html`, sorted by next editorial action while preserving `POST /operators/manuscripts` as the JSON draft-creation endpoint. Drafts are also ranked by publication distance at `/operators/publication-candidates` and `/operators/publication-candidates.json`, with workbench field `publicationCandidates` and MCP tool `read_publication_candidates`; this is a projection over standards reviews and source-cycle repair packets, not a new task store. A standards-clear draft with an open source-cycle repair packet is now an `editorial-hold` candidate with links to the repair packet, so source/reference/provenance work remains...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): `content/posts/acurite-delays-app-migration-due-to-user-concerns-over.md` was published through the new newsroom HTML publish flow on 2026-06-19 and the public site was redeployed. It now fails standards for malformed H2 headings and remains a revision target for no internal links, one reference, uncited bibliography, SEO-term overuse, and malformed H2 headings. `content/posts/uk-retail-sales-spike-masks-climate-driven-supply-fragility.md` (2026-06-19) was generated earlier by the source cycle from Koios's `collapse-finance` wire, human-edited for standards, converted to canonical CSL `references:` with `issued.date-parts`, and published through the standards gate after redeploy. It has e...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): ` body starts, then published through `POST /operators/manuscripts/world-cup-faces-air-quality-disaster-as-wildfires-threaten/publish` on 2026-06-19. It has `referenceCount: 2`, `draft: false`, `gates_clear: true`, and no standards findings. `content/posts/amazon-s-astro-and-the-energy-cost-of-giving.md` was generated by the 2026-06-19 source cycle from the `collapse-tech` wire after 19 new source records were written. The wrapper normalized two intake URLs into canonical CSL `references:` and added draft same-site link guidance. Human editing tightened the description and changed one H2 to include `AI infrastructure energy use`, then the article was published through `POST /operators/man...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): The `hello-world` article renders that A-Ads iframe from frontmatter under the fingerprint/banner image, and the public CSP/privacy policy recognize the A-Ads frame. - **The content and data stores are submodules.** Posts live in `content/posts/`; data lives in `data/`, with append-only traces under `data/logs/` and current JSON records under `data/records/`. - **The producing-content instance is configured in** `producing-content.config.json`: Koios's farm property, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, model, content store, and data store. `package.json` is package/site metadata, not the farm instance carrier. The seed vocabulary now ex...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): - The manual workshop references `scripts/new-post.mjs` and `scripts/lint-post.mjs`, but no `scripts/` directory is present in this package. Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes. - The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand. - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK re...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes. - The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand. - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the lat...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before dr...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting. - The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream wor...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Internal planning now has `src/administrating/developing`, exposed in `/departments.json`, human board `/administrating/developing`, `/administrating/developing.json`, OpenAPI, and MCP tool `read_developing`. Its `topologyConstruction` section records the current upstream/content-derived plan for nested component topology: source-to-reference fibration, desk-chain nesting, article sheaf/cosheaf, newsroom swarm hands, semantic hypertext packets, and aperiodic dream budgeting. Semantic hypertext packets are now active for source-cycle repair packets and content revision packets through `.packet.html` routes with scriptless `data-e`/`data-a`/`data-v` cells. Aggregate ad-performance managemen...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Draft-time same-site link guidance now sits before publication: `/operators/